  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 725 AM CDT Wed Aug 19 2026\n\nToday and Tomorrow...\n\nWater vapor imagery and objective analysis this morning depict an \namplifying ridge over the western CONUS, bringing northwesterly flow \naloft to much of the area. A train of shortwave disturbances passing \nthrough the flow will bring near daily storm chances, the first of \nwhich is passing through this morning/afternoon. \n\nStorm development has trended later than initial CAM guidance this \nmorning, allowing for additional (though still not overly \nimpressive) instability, with pockets of 1500-2000 J/kg of MUCAPE in \nplace. An additional delayed trend will bring more instability\nto work with. A cluster of thunderstorms is expected to develop\nacross southeast SD/northeast NE later this morning before \npushing southward across eastern NE/far western IA through the \nremainder of the morning and early afternoon. Sufficient bulk \nshear (40-50 kts) will be in place with model hodographs being \nlong and generally straight. However, 0-3 km shear remains \nweaker (~15 kts), bringing the potential for an initial hail \nthreat before storms gust out and transition to more of a \ndamaging wind threat with pockets of 60-70 mph gusts possible. \n\nThe cluster of thunderstorms is expected to exit to the south by mid-\nafternoon. CAM guidance indicates some potential for storms to \nredevelop across eastern Nebraska during the late afternoon and \nearly evening, though this potential will hinder on the amount of \ndestabilization that is able to occur behind the initial cluster of \nthunderstorms. \n\nCloud cover will break up and improve through the evening, setting \nthe stage for fog development overnight. Model soundings indicate \ncalm winds and a saturated surface layer, though somewhat shallow, \nit should allow for fairly widespread fog with patchy areas of dense \nfog Thursday morning before gradually burning up after sunrise. High \ntemperatures on Thursday will be near seasonal norms, peaking in the \nmid 80s. \n\nLater Thursday, surface low pressure will develop over Minnesota, \ndraping a cold front southwestward into northeast Nebraska. \nDestabilization through the afternoon will bring a corridor of ample \ninstability (MLCAPE > 2500 J/kg) ahead of the front in northeast NE. \nCoupled with sufficient bulk shear, strong to severe storm \ndevelopment will be possible along the front during the late \nafternoon and evening. The primary question will be how widespread \ndevelopment along the front is, with the better forcing for ascent \nto the northeast and a weak cap in place. If storms are able to \ninitiate in the area, model soundings indicate steep lapse rates and \na favorable environment for isolated pockets of large hail and \nstrong wind gusts. For the time being, PoPs are capped at 20% given \nthe lower confidence in convective initiation and likely more \nisolated nature of development. \n\nFriday and Beyond...\n\nFriday into early next week, the large scale pattern will remain \ngenerally unchanged, with ridging centered over the Four Corners \nregion and a stream of shortwave disturbances embedded in the \nnorthwesterly flow aloft bringing periodic precipitation chances. \nHigh temperatures on Friday are expected in the mid to upper 80s \nbefore storm chances return again during the evening. An unstable \nairmass will develop through the afternoon before an incoming \nshortwave disturbance and surface trough initiate a round of strong \nto severe storms over western Nebraska, gradually pushing into the \narea through the evening and early overnight period. Questions \nremain regarding storm coverage, with PoPs of 30-45% in place.\n\nHigh temperatures through the weekend and early next week will \noscillate from the upper 70s to the mid 80s as daily precipitation \nchances and cloud cover occasionally hinder daytime heating. Near-\ndaily PoPs of 15-30% are in place, with additional strong to severe \nstorms possible.",
